        "id": 1881060
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-06-21T21:25:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e  So basically you can make the tests agnostic of the underlying set of flags by saying \"run combinations of all these flags that I claim impact my source code,\" huh?\n\nCorrect\n\n\u003e And then what, do you change assert statements based on the flag value if you need?\n\nThis is a possibility, but if there is a big behavior change, it is better to write a test with flag off and one with flag on, and annotate them with @EnableFlags or @DisableFlag. This will work smartly with the parametric test and skip the test when appropriate\n\n\u003e  I feel like this can quickly cause the number of tests run to get a bit out of control\n\nYes, that why you need to manually enable it.\n\n\n\u003e  One could argue that that is testing an implementation detail though.\n\nSo let\u0027s put a parametric over all the test so we are sure that it works everytime. \nTo me, that sound like a good usage.\n\nThe example I put are exactly that, in one I am changing a detail on how the bluetooth is killed, on the other Henri is changing an internal detail of the threading model, without consequence on the tests.",
      "parentUuid": "fc2cea8d_92cfe342",
      "range": {
